<!--
    kuna issue #762 (P6 variables, naming-policy; P9 emission, declaration
    emission).  Two distinct pointer locals both took the name `object` from
    the parameter of the callee they are passed to, and `dedupvardecls` then
    collapsed their two identical declarations into one, so the emitted C read
    and wrote one variable where the binary keeps two.  The C compiles, but the
    store after the inner loop hits the last member instead of the retained
    representative.

    Source (original synthetic C, gcc 13.3 -O2 -fPIC, consume in its own
    translation unit):

      struct Node { unsigned char skip; unsigned int hits; };
      void consume(struct Node *object) { ++object->hits; }
      void visit(struct Node **representatives, unsigned int representative_count,
                 struct Node **members, unsigned int member_count) {
          for (unsigned int i = 0; i < representative_count; ++i) {
              struct Node *representative = representatives[i];
              consume(representative);
              representative->skip = 1;
              for (unsigned int j = 0; j < member_count; ++j) {
                  struct Node *member = members[j];
                  if (!member) break;
                  if (!member->skip) consume(member);
              }
              representative->skip = 0;
          }
      }

    The representative lives in r12 across the inner loop, the member in rdi.
    Before the fix every pass printed `object = *v2;` inside the loop and one
    `Node *object;` declaration, so `object->skip = 0` stored through the member.

    The naming fix: upstream gives every named high its own local Symbol, so
    `lookForFuncParamNames`' `makeNameUnique` sees the first `object` and the
    second becomes `object_00`.  kuna binds these names on the HighVariable
    without a Symbol, so the uniqueness check now also consults the names
    already bound in the pass.  The declaration fix: the identical-line collapse
    only merges lines of one object (one bound or referenced Symbol), since a
    shared name is not evidence of a shared identity.  Neither is a judgment
    call, so there is no option; `dedupvardecls off` must print the same two
    variables.

    Pass 1 (default), pass 2 (option dedupvardecls off) and pass 3 (option
    namestyle ghidra) all declare both variables, keep the member in its own
    variable, and store the cleared flag through the representative.
-->
